  "biography": {
    "plain_text": "Gary Primm is an American casino developer who inherited Whiskey Pete's from his father, Ernest, and expanded the family's established Nevada business with Primm Valley and Buffalo Bill's. He also developed New York-New York in Las Vegas with Kirk Kerkorian before selling his casino properties to MGM Grand in 1999.",
    "confidence": {
      "score": 94,
      "band": "high",
      "reason": "Reliable business history supports the concise inheritance, expansion and sale account."
    },
    "source_ids": [
      "S1"
    ],
    "word_count": 50,
    "html": "<p>Gary Primm is an American casino developer who inherited Whiskey Pete's from his father, Ernest, and expanded the family's established Nevada business with Primm Valley and Buffalo Bill's. He also developed New York-New York in Las Vegas with Kirk Kerkorian before selling his casino properties to MGM Grand in 1999.</p>\r\n"
  },
  "long_biography": {
    "plain_text": "At Nevada's Primadonna casino, Gary Primm gave his interests in flying and cars a public setting. The casino displayed model biplanes, balloons and racing cars overhead, while its murals featured aircraft and wingwalkers. The Skydiver Restaurant carried the aviation theme into flight-jacket-shaped menus and booths sheltered by parachute canopies.\n\nHis collecting also supplied a separate attraction at Whiskey Pete's. In 1989, Primm bought the 1934 Ford associated with Bonnie and Clyde from a Reno collector. The car went on display alongside a copy of a letter attributed to Clyde Barrow praising Ford's vehicles, bringing a piece of American criminal history into the casino.",
    "confidence": {
      "score": 91,
      "band": "high",
      "reason": "Contemporary state magazine relevant full article section read; specific interiors and collector acquisition establish an independent layer."
    },
    "source_ids": [
      "S2"
    ],
    "word_count": 103,
    "html": "<p>At Nevada's Primadonna casino, Gary Primm gave his interests in flying and cars a public setting. The casino displayed model biplanes, balloons and racing cars overhead, while its murals featured aircraft and wingwalkers. The Skydiver Restaurant carried the aviation theme into flight-jacket-shaped menus and booths sheltered by parachute canopies.</p>\r\n<p>His collecting also supplied a separate attraction at Whiskey Pete's. In 1989, Primm bought the 1934 Ford associated with Bonnie and Clyde from a Reno collector. The car went on display alongside a copy of a letter attributed to Clyde Barrow praising Ford's vehicles, bringing a piece of American criminal history into the casino.</p>\r\n"
  },
